 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Discrete Lagrangian Optimization for Fixed Partitions
-A variant that addresses the memory-constrained optimization problem for fixed partitions. It converts the constrained problem into an unconstrained discrete Lagrangian problem using a Lagrangian multiplier $\lambda \ge 0$ [S1, unofficial]. The complexity is reduced from exponential to linear by exploiting the separability of the problem, allowing independent minimization of terms for each block $B_k$ [S1, unofficial].
+A variant that optimizes the assignment of extraction methods (e.g., in-core vs. out-of-core) to blocks under a memory constraint [S2, unofficial]. It converts the constrained optimization problem into an unconstrained discrete Lagrangian problem using a Lagrangian multiplier $\lambda \ge 0$ [S2, unofficial]. The complexity is reduced to linear by exploiting separability, minimizing terms independently for each block $B_k$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The expected time to read interval tree blocks is proportional to the sum of probabilities of block intervals. The expected time to read min/max list blocks is approximated by a term dependent on the total probability and block capacity, plus a term dependent on cell intervals [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** The method is designed for out-of-core operation, storing the interval tree and min/max lists on disk. Only necessary blocks are loaded into memory, allowing processing of datasets larger than available RAM [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Experimental results on datasets ranging from 1 to 250 MB showed that the probabilistic estimation reduced compute time by a factor between 20 and 100, with larger savings for larger datasets [S1, unofficial]. The error of estimated expected extraction times was typically less than five percent [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Enables isosurface extraction for very large datasets that do not fit in main memory. Efficient I/O by loading only necessary blocks [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on accurate probabilistic estimation of isovalue distributions. Performance depends on disk I/O speed ($\alpha_6$). The optimization problem for partitioning can be complex, though it can be linearized using Lagrangian multipliers [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Suitable for large volume datasets that exceed available main memory [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for small datasets where in-core methods are faster due to lower overhead. Not suitable if accurate probability distributions are unavailable [S1, unofficial].
+*   **I/O Complexity:** Optimal $O(\log N + K)$ I/O operations for stabbing queries, where $N$ is the number of blocks and $K$ is the number of active cells [S1]. Specifically, $O(T/B + \log_B N)$ where $T$ is active blocks and $B$ is block size [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Expected time to read interval tree blocks: $T_1 = \alpha_6 \sum_{I^0} \sum_{i=1}^{N_B} Pr(I(B_i))$ [S2, unofficial]. Expected time to read min/max lists: $T_2 \approx \alpha_6 \frac{Pr(I)}{K_2} + \sum_{I_{cell}} Pr(I_\eta) \frac{1}{2}$ [S2, unofficial]. Total expected cell extraction time: $E[T(B_\nu, 4, t)] = E[T(B_\nu, 2, t)] + T_1 + T_2$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Usage:** Minimal main memory requirement, limited to cache size and tree traversal stack. Scales to datasets much larger than RAM [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles massive datasets; I/O optimal; parallelizable [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** High overhead for small datasets; disk I/O latency can dominate if cache misses are frequent; requires careful tuning of $K_1, K_2$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Large datasets that do not fit in memory; parallel clusters with local disk storage [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Small datasets; environments with very slow disk I/O; real-time applications with strict latency constraints [S1][S2, unofficial].
+
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of optimal hybrid memory constrained isosurface extraction [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the challenge of processing large volume datasets that cannot fit entirely in main memory by partitioning the interval tree and min/max lists into disk blocks. The approach uses probabilistic estimation to predict I/O costs and optimize block loading [S1, unofficial].
+The method was devised by Chiang, Silva, and Schroeder to overcome memory bottlenecks in isosurface extraction for large volume datasets [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of external interval trees for optimal I/O performance [S1]. It is used in parallel and out-of-core isocontouring on commodity clusters [S1].
